   "text": "Skip to Main Content\nGo to TN.gov\nGo to TN.gov\nGo to Environment & Conservation (TDEC)\nProgram Areas Permitting Public Participation About Contact Calendar of Events American Rescue Plan (ARP) TDEC Newsroom\nPrint This Page\nWE ARE REORGANIZING PAGES FOR A BETTER EXPERIENCE. WE APPRECIATE YOUR PATIENCE!\nCumberland Plateau\nBig Bone Cave\nColditz Cove\nChimneys\nFall Creek Falls\nFalling Water Falls\nGrundy Forest\nHawkins Cove\nHicks Gap\nHoney Creek\nHubbard’s Cave\nLaurel-Snow\nLost Creek\nMr. and Mrs. Harry Lee Carter\nNatural Bridge\nNorth Chickamauga Creek Gorge\nOzone Falls\nPiney Falls\nPogue Creek Canyon\nRugby\nSavage Gulf\nSherwood Forest\nStinging Fork Falls\nTwin Arches\nVirgin Falls\nWalls of Jericho\nMr. and Mrs. Harry Lee Carter\nClass II Natural-Scientific State Natural Area\nReturn to Cumberland Plateau\nThe Mr. and Mrs. Harry Lee Carter State Natural Area is a 931-acre natural area located in Franklin County that is part of the South Cumberland Recreation Area. Named after the couple who donated the land to the State, this natural area protects part of a large solution valley associated with the karst erosional processes characteristic of the Cumberland Plateau escarpment. A significant cave system extends from Lost Cove to the head of Crow Creek. The stream systems draining into Lost Cove disappear into the Lost Cove Cave at the Big Sinks and travel underground for over a mile, emerging at the main entrance Buggytop Cave. This impressive cave entrance is 100 feet wide and 80 feet high. Peter Cave is the other main cave entrance. There have been considerable archeological artifacts of the Woodland and Mississippian period excavated from the entrance. The artifacts are on display at the University of the South in Sewanee.\nThe cave environment is fragile. Visitors that choose to explore the cave should be careful not to disturb any formations or small animals that may be seen. The area is also home to several rare species of plants, animals and unique plant communities. Examples of rare plants include Cumberland rosinweed (Silphium brachiatum) and Eggert's sunflower (Helianthus eggertii). The forested area abounds with oaks, hickories, maples, and many shrubs. A rich mixed mesophytic forest is found in the cove near the cave entrance. This forest community has numerous dominant species that include sugar maple, buckeye, tulip poplar, buckeye, and basswood. Many of the slopes and spurs are an oak-hickory forest community with white oak prevalent on mid slopes and scarlet, black, and chestnut oak occurring on drier upper slopes. A new forest is forming here to replace the forest removed by past logging activities. There is also a limestone barrens community above the Buggytop Cave entrance that is home to native grasses and other barrens plants like liatris, rattlesnake master, and agave. The natural area has a wonderful spring wildflower display that adds much seasonal color to the forest floor.\nSite Management\nSouth Cumberland Recreation Area\n11745 US 41\nMonteagle, TN 37356\n(931) 924-2980.\nDivision of Natural Areas\nDavy Crockett Tower\n500 James Robertson Parkway\n8th Floor, Nashville, TN 37243\n(615) 532-0431\nPublic Access\nOpen to the Public - Full Access. Parking and hiking trails are provided. The natural area is operated by Tennessee State Parks. There is a parking area and a 2-mile (one way) hiking trail provided. Cave tours are available.\nParking: Yes\nTrail: Yes\nDogs on leash: Yes\nHunting: No\nFishing: No\nCamping: No\nDirections\nTo Mr. and Mrs. Harry Lee Carter Natural Area: take exit 134 off I-24, turn left to Sewanee, then turn left on Highway 56. Continue 10 miles to the parking area located on the left. To South Cumberland Recreation Area: take exit 134 off I-24 and turn right to Monteagle then turn left on Highway 41/56 to Tracy City. Continue for three miles to the visitor center located on the left.\nDetails & Map\nMap of Mr. and Mrs. Harry Lee Carter State Natural Area (Georeferenced PDF)\nOwned by the State of Tennessee\nSinking Cove & Sewanee 7.5-minute quadrangles\nCumberland Plateau Physiographic Province\nDesignated in 1975\nSocial Media\nfacebook youtube linkedin\nPrograms\nAir\nEnvironmental Stewardship\nSolid Waste\nUnderground Storage Tanks\nWater\nResources\nAccessibility\nDataviewers\nEmployment and Internships\nEnforcement Orders\nGrants\nNon-Discrimination\nFeatured Links\nTDEC Annual Reports\nField Offices\nNatural Areas\nState Parks\nThe Tennessee Conservationist\nPartner Links\nTN Historical Commission\nSustainable Tourism\nTN Promise\nU.S. EPA\nDepartment of Environment & Conservation\nDavid W. Salyers, P.E., P.G.\nDavy Crockett Tower\n500 James Robertson Parkway\nNashville, TN 37243\n(888) 891-TDEC (8332)\nEmail Questions to TDEC\nFlag Status: Full Staff Chat\nHelp Translate Font Size\nTN.gov Services TN.gov Directory Transparent TN Web Policies Mobile App\nAbout Tennessee Title VI Accessibility Help & Contact Survey",
